    <!-- ── 04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact ──────────────────── -->
    <section class="weekly-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="weekly-secnum">04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact</div>
        <h2>A denied response needs downstream evidence</h2>
        <p>The Google Cloud author correlated runtime events with downstream request logs, rather than relying on the model's refusal. This demonstrates tool-level denial in that lab, not exact-payload approval, reviewer authority verification, or single-use authorization.<sup><a href="#s7">7</a></sup></p>
        <p>Decision Governance is the category. The Decision Authority Layer is the runtime enforcement layer. DAL-X is the application Jochanni Labs is building. Its published simulation describes payload-bound signed tokens, reviewer-membership and authority-freshness checks, and rejection of invalid or consumed tokens.<sup><a href="#s8">8</a></sup></p>
        <p><strong>Conditional prevention:</strong> this design could stop an owner-only wallet transfer requested by a public caller if enterprise trigger logic selects it, valid authority binds the exact action and payload, and the downstream system rejects missing or invalid authority across every relevant execution path. It cannot control a public blockchain merely because DAL-X exists. An agent retaining an independently usable signing key could bypass a protected service.</p>

        <p class="weekly-status">DAL-X currently runs in a simulated enterprise environment. Production enforcement controls are still being completed. No live enterprise deployment is established.<sup><a href="#s9">9</a></sup></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 05 / Enterprise Check and Next Week's Watch ──────────────── -->
    <section class="weekly-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="weekly-secnum">05 / Enterprise Check and Next Week's Watch</div>
        <h2>Test hidden tools and changed ledger edits</h2>
        <p><strong>Wallet transfer:</strong> request an owner-only tool from a public context. Test the defect where hiding a tool substitutes for executor authorization. Effective control requires an executor denial and downstream evidence that no transaction was broadcast.</p>
        <p><strong>Closed-period edit:</strong> approve a proposal, change its payload, and retry through direct API and batch paths. Require rejection receipts and matching ledger evidence. A review record without rejection does not establish control.</p>
        <p>Watch for an IntentKit fixed release and ServiceNow implementation evidence. Both remain unresolved in the reviewed sources.</p>

        <div class="weekly-question">Can your receiving system reject the exact action when the agent has access but lacks authority to execute it?</div>

